Lesson Summary
Identifying security threats includes identifying types of attackers, social engineering attacks, malware, as well as software-based, network-based, wireless and physical threats.
A hacker/attacker is an individual who has the skills to gain access to computer systems through unauthorized or unapproved means.
A cracker is an individual who breaks encryption codes, defeats software copy protections, or specializes in breaking into systems.
A threat actor is an entity that is partially or wholly responsible for an accident that affects or potentially affects an organization's security. Types of threat actors are "script kiddie", "hacktivist", "organized crime", "nation states", "insiders" and "competitors".
Social engineering refers to attacking people, targeting individuals.
A virus is a piece of malicious code that spreads from one computer to another by attaching itself to other files through a process of self-replication.
Some of the types of password attacks are guessing, stealing, dictionary attack, brute force attack, and rainbow table attack.
TCP/IP Basics is an open protocol that is supported by every major network operating system.
Physical threats and vulnerabilities refer to unauthorised access to facilities and hardware, environmental issues that cause damage, as well as equipment theft.


